{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795847153,"nonce":"7Tm2QIPX5D/LARVdiP3JRskGD+Q=","title":"Experiment: a Stateright model of the gate and rerun state machine","message":"Owner asked for this ticket on 2026-11-28, after asking whether the project needs Lean proofs. The answer given was no to Lean (a proof is about a model, nothing ties it to the Rust), and yes to trying a model checker on the parts whose bugs are about interleavings. This is the smallest experiment that would show whether it pays.\n\n## What to build\nA Stateright model (Stateright is a model checker written in Rust) of the gate and rerun state machine: Steps, needs, gates (manual, Environment approval, fork approval), Takes, shared services, and the actions create, tick, release, reject, expire, rerun, retry.\n\nOne invariant to start with: nothing of a fork-code Run (no Step, no shared service) runs before a release of its fork approval gate. Add: an Environment approval gate and a fork approval gate never stand in for each other; no Run deadlocks with a Step waiting on a service that never starts.\n\n## How to judge it\nReviewers found these by reading the code in the 2026-11-28 session. Seed each one back into the model (or check the model against the code before each fix) and see whether the checker finds it:\n1. A shared service starts before release when the Step using it is a root excluded by when: (the gate was not in front of excluded roots).\n2. A rerun of a when:-excluded root runs it ungated; a rerun of a Step below an excluded Step runs the excluded Step.\n3. A rerun that fails partway leaves the gate pending, and a repeated rerun runs the target while the gate waits.\n4. A service's readiness deadline keeps running while it is held, so it fails right after release; and a service launched before release gets its deadline restarted.\n5. A skipped fork gate holds services forever.\n\nIf the model finds most of them with a small state space and little modelling effort, it is worth keeping in the repo and extending. If it needs so much abstraction that it no longer resembles the scheduler, stop and write down why.\n\n## Out of scope for the experiment\n- Lease and term fencing across replicas (ADR-0077). It is the better long-term candidate for a model checker, and the chaos tier already attacks it at runtime; pick it up only if this experiment pays.\n- OAuth refresh rotation (the thief-stays-in-step case), a second candidate.\n- Anything that depends on Kubernetes or forge behaviour, where the risk is the outside world differing from the model.\n- Property-based tests are enough for the pure functions: the OIDC subject string (no field can read as another segment) and operation-list intersection for delegated calls.\n\n## Open points\n- Where the model lives (a crate under crates/, dev-only, not in the default workspace build?) and whether it runs in CI.\n- How to keep the model from drifting from the scheduler: shared types, or a documented mapping.\n- This direction was an opinion from one session's findings and has not been audited; audit it before committing more than the experiment.\n\nCode to read first: crates/scarab-engine/src/scheduler.rs (fork_approval_hold, the rerun plan), crates/scarab-policy/src/fork_run_approval_gate.rs and approval_gate.rs, on the branch that carries Fork Run approval.","files":null}]}